    questions: [
      { question: "Why is my ALFA AC1900 capped at 40 Mbps on a gigabit network?", answer: "The adapter is almost certainly in a USB 2.0 port, which has a 480 Mbps ceiling. USB 2.0 overhead and protocol inefficiency usually cap real Wi-Fi speed around 100-150 Mbps. Move the adapter to a blue USB 3.0 port." },
      { question: "Does the ALFA AC1900 work on Linux?", answer: "Yes, but you must build the Realtek RTL8814AU driver for your kernel. The Aircrack-ng repository has a maintained driver. The adapter will not work with the generic in-kernel driver." },
      { question: "Will a USB 3.0 extension cable slow down the ALFA AC1900?", answer: "A short high-quality USB 3.0 extension cable will not significantly affect speed. A long or cheap cable, or one labeled 'USB 2.0', will drop the adapter to USB 2.0 speed and cap throughput." },
      { question: "Why does Windows prefer the onboard Wi-Fi over the ALFA adapter?", answer: "Windows assigns a metric to each network adapter and prefers the one with the lowest value. The onboard adapter usually has a lower metric. Disable the onboard adapter in Device Manager or manually set the ALFA's metric lower in the adapter's advanced properties." }
    ]
